## Well-Structured Unit Test Class

```java
class OrderServiceTest {

    // Dependencies — mocked
    @Mock
    private OrderRepository orderRepo;
    @Mock
    private PaymentGateway paymentGateway;
    @Mock
    private EmailService emailService;

    @InjectMocks
    private OrderService orderService;  // Class under test

    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() {
        MockitoAnnotations.openMocks(this);  // Or use @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
    }

    @Nested
    @DisplayName("createOrder")
    class CreateOrder {

        @Test
        @DisplayName("should create order and send confirmation email")
        void shouldCreateOrderAndSendEmail() {
            // ARRANGE
            CreateOrderRequest request = buildValidRequest();
            Order savedOrder = buildSavedOrder(1L);
            when(orderRepo.save(any(Order.class))).thenReturn(savedOrder);

            // ACT
            Order result = orderService.createOrder(request);

            // ASSERT
            assertThat(result.getId()).isEqualTo(1L);
            assertThat(result.getStatus()).isEqualTo(OrderStatus.PENDING);
            verify(emailService).sendOrderConfirmation(savedOrder);
        }

        @Test
        @DisplayName("should throw exception when items list is empty")
        void shouldThrowWhenItemsEmpty() {
            CreateOrderRequest request = new CreateOrderRequest();
            request.setItems(Collections.emptyList());

            assertThatThrownBy(() -> orderService.createOrder(request))
                .isInstanceOf(InvalidOrderException.class)
                .hasMessageContaining("items");
        }
    }

    @Nested
    @DisplayName("cancelOrder")
    class CancelOrder {

        @Test
        @DisplayName("should cancel a pending order")
        void shouldCancelPendingOrder() {
            Order order = buildOrderWithStatus(OrderStatus.PENDING);
            when(orderRepo.findById(1L)).thenReturn(Optional.of(order));

            orderService.cancelOrder(1L);

            assertThat(order.getStatus()).isEqualTo(OrderStatus.CANCELLED);
            verify(orderRepo).save(order);
        }

        @Test
        @DisplayName("should throw when trying to cancel a shipped order")
        void shouldThrowWhenCancellingShippedOrder() {
            Order order = buildOrderWithStatus(OrderStatus.SHIPPED);
            when(orderRepo.findById(1L)).thenReturn(Optional.of(order));

            assertThatThrownBy(() -> orderService.cancelOrder(1L))
                .isInstanceOf(InvalidOrderStateException.class);
        }
    }

    // Parameterized test for different inputs:
    @ParameterizedTest(name = "status {0} should be cancelable: {1}")
    @CsvSource({
        "PENDING, true",
        "PROCESSING, true",
        "SHIPPED, false",
        "DELIVERED, false",
        "CANCELLED, false"
    })
    void cancelability(OrderStatus status, boolean expectedCancelable) {
        Order order = buildOrderWithStatus(status);
        assertThat(orderService.canBeCancelled(order)).isEqualTo(expectedCancelable);
    }

    // Test data builders:
    private CreateOrderRequest buildValidRequest() {
        CreateOrderRequest req = new CreateOrderRequest();
        req.setUserId(42L);
        req.setItems(List.of(new OrderItemRequest(1L, 2)));
        return req;
    }
}
```

## AssertJ — Fluent Assertions

```java
// JUnit built-in (verbose):
assertEquals("PENDING", order.getStatus().name());
assertTrue(order.getItems().size() > 0);
assertNull(order.getShippedAt());

// AssertJ (fluent, readable, better error messages):
assertThat(order.getStatus()).isEqualTo(OrderStatus.PENDING);
assertThat(order.getItems()).isNotEmpty().hasSize(2);
assertThat(order.getShippedAt()).isNull();
assertThat(order.getTotal()).isGreaterThan(BigDecimal.ZERO);
assertThat(result).extracting(Order::getId, Order::getStatus)
    .containsExactly(42L, OrderStatus.PENDING);

// Collection assertions:
assertThat(orders)
    .hasSize(3)
    .extracting(Order::getStatus)
    .containsOnly(OrderStatus.PENDING, OrderStatus.PROCESSING);

// Exception assertions:
assertThatThrownBy(() -> service.findById(-1L))
    .isInstanceOf(InvalidIdException.class)
    .hasMessage("ID must be positive")
    .hasNoCause();

// Soft assertions — collect all failures:
SoftAssertions soft = new SoftAssertions();
soft.assertThat(order.getStatus()).isEqualTo(OrderStatus.PENDING);
soft.assertThat(order.getTotal()).isEqualByComparingTo("99.99");
soft.assertAll();  // Reports all failures together
```

## Test Naming Conventions

```java
// Pattern: should[ExpectedBehavior]When[Condition]
@Test
void shouldThrowOrderNotFoundWhenIdDoesNotExist() { ... }

// Pattern: given[Context]_when[Action]_then[Outcome]
@Test
void givenPendingOrder_whenCancelCalled_thenStatusIsCancelled() { ... }

// Pattern: [action][Expected result][Description]
@Test
void createOrder_returnsCreatedOrder_withPendingStatus() { ... }

// With @DisplayName — human readable test names in report:
@Test
@DisplayName("Given an order with SHIPPED status, when cancel is called, should throw InvalidOrderStateException")
void test1() { ... }
```

## Production Reality

Aim for tests that are: Fast (milliseconds — no DB, no network), Isolated (no shared state between tests), Repeatable (same result every run), Self-validating (pass/fail clearly determined), and Timely (written alongside or before the code — FIRST acronym). A test suite that's slow or flaky gets skipped. The value of tests comes from running them constantly.

## What to Test in a Service Method

```java
// Service method to test:
public Order createOrder(CreateOrderRequest req, String username) {
    // 1. Validate user exists
    User user = userRepo.findByUsername(username)
        .orElseThrow(() -> new UserNotFoundException(username));

    // 2. Check user is not suspended
    if (user.isSuspended()) {
        throw new UserSuspendedException(username);
    }

    // 3. Check item stock
    for (OrderItemRequest item : req.getItems()) {
        Product product = productRepo.findById(item.getProductId())
            .orElseThrow(() -> new ProductNotFoundException(item.getProductId()));
        if (product.getStock() < item.getQuantity()) {
            throw new InsufficientStockException(product.getId(), item.getQuantity(), product.getStock());
        }
    }

    // 4. Calculate total and apply promo
    BigDecimal total = calculateTotal(req.getItems());
    if (req.getPromoCode() != null) {
        total = promoService.apply(req.getPromoCode(), total);
    }

    // 5. Save and notify
    Order order = new Order(user, req.getItems(), total);
    Order saved = orderRepo.save(order);
    eventPublisher.publishEvent(new OrderCreatedEvent(saved));
    return saved;
}

// Test cases needed:
// Happy path: all valid → order created, event published
// Error: user not found → UserNotFoundException
// Error: user is suspended → UserSuspendedException
// Error: product not found → ProductNotFoundException
// Error: insufficient stock → InsufficientStockException
// Branch: promo code present → promoService called; no promo → not called
// Total 6-7 tests covering all branches
```

## Boundary Condition Testing

```java
@ParameterizedTest
@MethodSource("discountBoundaryProvider")
void shouldApplyCorrectDiscount(BigDecimal orderTotal, BigDecimal expectedDiscount) {
    // Discount tiers: <$50=0%, $50-$100=5%, >$100=10%
    BigDecimal actualDiscount = discountCalculator.calculate(orderTotal);
    assertThat(actualDiscount).isEqualByComparingTo(expectedDiscount);
}

static Stream<Arguments> discountBoundaryProvider() {
    return Stream.of(
        Arguments.of(BigDecimal.valueOf(49.99), BigDecimal.ZERO),      // Below threshold
        Arguments.of(BigDecimal.valueOf(50.00), BigDecimal.valueOf(5)), // Exact boundary
        Arguments.of(BigDecimal.valueOf(50.01), BigDecimal.valueOf(5)), // Just above
        Arguments.of(BigDecimal.valueOf(99.99), BigDecimal.valueOf(5)), // Just below next
        Arguments.of(BigDecimal.valueOf(100.00), BigDecimal.TEN),      // Next boundary
        Arguments.of(BigDecimal.valueOf(200.00), BigDecimal.TEN)       // Well above
    );
}
```

## What NOT to Test

```java
// Don't test: Lombok-generated code
@Data
public class Order {
    private Long id;  // Don't test getId(), setId() — Lombok generates them
}

// Don't test: Simple delegation with no logic
public OrderResponse getOrder(Long id) {
    return orderMapper.toResponse(orderRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow());
    // The mapping and findById are unit tested elsewhere
    // This thin wrapper doesn't add enough logic to warrant its own test
    // → Cover via integration test
}

// Don't test: Spring @Configuration classes
// → Spring's own tests cover bean creation

// DO test: Business logic regardless of complexity appearance
public OrderStatus determineNextStatus(Order order) {
    // Complex state machine — MUST test all transitions
    return switch (order.getStatus()) {
        case PENDING -> order.isPaymentConfirmed() ? PROCESSING : PENDING;
        case PROCESSING -> order.isShipped() ? SHIPPED : PROCESSING;
        // ...
    };
}
```

## Production Reality

Aim for meaningful test coverage of business logic — typically 80%+ coverage of service/domain classes. Use JaCoCo in CI to track coverage trends, not as an absolute gate. A coverage decrease of >5% in a PR should trigger review. Exclude: generated code (Lombok, MapStruct, QueryDSL), configuration classes, simple DTOs, and exception classes from coverage requirements.
